---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import EmergencyFundLab from "../../components/EmergencyFundLab.tsx";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Big Should Your Emergency Fund Be?"
  description="Size an emergency fund from your essential expenses and how risky your income is — steady salary vs. variable, one income vs. two, dependents — then see how long to fund it."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Personal Finance · Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>How big should your emergency fund be?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      An emergency fund is the shock absorber of a financial life: cash set aside so
      a job loss or a surprise bill doesn't turn into credit-card debt — or force you
      to sell investments at the worst possible moment. "Three to six months of
      expenses" is the rule of thumb, but the right number depends on your situation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <EmergencyFundLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why the size varies</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>How steady your income is.</strong> A tenured salary can lean toward
          three months; commission, gig, freelance, or business income swings more, so
          it needs a deeper cushion — six months or more.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How many incomes support the household.</strong> Two earners cushion
          each other; if one loses work, the other keeps things afloat. A single income
          carries all the risk, so it warrants a larger fund.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who depends on you.</strong> Dependents raise the cost of an
          interruption and lower your flexibility to cut back, so the buffer should be
          bigger.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lumpy one-off risks.</strong> A fund isn't only about lost income —
          it also absorbs surprise costs: a health-plan deductible, a major home
          repair, an emergency vet bill. The optional buffer adds for the ones you're
          exposed to, which is why even a rock-solid job might still want a larger
          fund.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Where to keep it</h2>
      <p>
        The emergency fund's job is <em>safety and access</em>, not growth. Keep it in
        a high-yield savings account or a money-market fund — somewhere it won't drop
        20% right when you need it. It's the one pot of money that shouldn't be
        invested in stocks. Once it's full, that's your green light to point new
        savings at debt payoff and investing. Educational only, not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pf-related">
    <h2 class="pf-related-heading">Keep exploring</h2>
    <ul class="grid grid--2 pf-related-grid">
      <li>
        <a href="/personal-finance/budget" class="card">
          <h3>Find the money to fund it</h3>
          <p>The 50/30/20 budget shows how much of your take-home pay can go toward savings goals like this one.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/info/treasury-yields" class="card">
          <h3>Where safe cash earns</h3>
          <p>See what today's short-term rates look like — the yardstick for high-yield savings and money-market funds.</p>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .pf-related { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .pf-related-heading { font-size: var(--step-1); padding-bottom: var(--space-xs); border-bottom: var(--border); margin-bottom: var(--space-md); }
  .pf-related-grid { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .pf-related-grid a.card { height: 100%; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; display: block; }
  .pf-related-grid h3 { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; font-size: var(--step-1); }
  .pf-related-grid p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-soft); font-size: var(--step--1); }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.15rem; padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md); border-radius: var(--radius); background: var(--color-accent-soft); border: 1px solid var(--color-accent); }
  .ss-headline-label { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--color-text-soft); }
  .ss-headline-value { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--step-3); line-height: 1.1; color: var(--color-accent); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .ss-headline-sub { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--color-text-soft); }
  .ss-stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-sm); margin: 0 0 var(--space-md); }
  .ss-stats > div { background: var(--color-surface-alt); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: var(--space-sm); }
  .ss-stats dt { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--color-muted); margin-bottom: 0.2rem; }
  .ss-stats dd { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--step-0); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  .ef-progresslabel { font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-text-soft); margin: var(--space-md) 0 0.3rem; }
  .ef-bar { width: 100%; height: 1.4rem; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--color-surface-alt); overflow: hidden; border: var(--border); }
  .ef-fill { height: 100%; background: var(--color-accent); border-radius: 999px; transition: width var(--transition); }
</style>
